WEBVTT

00:00:00.136 --> 00:00:04.890
Telemetry is security-relevant data generated by systems, applications, identities,

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:07.000
networks, endpoints, and cloud services.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.020
It can include audit logs, authentication logs, endpoint events, network logs,

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:15.005
cloud activity records, vulnerability scan results, configuration snapshots,

00:00:15.005 --> 00:00:18.961
change records, tickets, incident records, and compliance evidence.

00:00:18.961 --> 00:00:21.000
Telemetry is the raw material of monitoring,

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.480
but raw data only becomes useful when it is reliable, understandable,

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:25.823
and available when needed.

00:00:25.823 --> 00:00:25.994
Source reliability matters.

00:00:25.994 --> 00:00:31.000
A log source that silently stops sending data can create a false sense of visibility.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.000
A scanner that misses important assets can distort vulnerability priorities.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.000
A ticket queue with inconsistent fields can weaken reporting.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.190
A configuration snapshot without timestamps or ownership may be hard to use as evidence.

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:45.000
Monitoring programs should track the health and quality of their evidence sources,

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.982
not only the events inside them.

00:00:46.982 --> 00:00:50.914
Retention and time synchronization are practical details with large consequences.

00:00:50.914 --> 00:00:53.845
Retention affects how far back investigators, auditors,

00:00:53.845 --> 00:00:56.000
and operators can reconstruct activity.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.985
Time synchronization affects whether events from different systems can be correlated.

00:00:59.985 --> 00:01:03.000
If authentication logs, cloud activity logs, endpoint events,

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.000
and tickets all use inconsistent clocks or short retention windows,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.405
the organization may struggle to understand what happened during an incident or audit

00:01:09.405 --> 00:01:10.215
review.

00:01:10.215 --> 00:01:13.000
Evidence should be current, traceable, and reviewable.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.014
Current means it reflects the system as it actually operates now.

00:01:16.014 --> 00:01:20.550
Traceable means a reviewer can connect evidence back to the asset, control, owner, event,

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:21.871
or decision it supports.

00:01:21.871 --> 00:01:25.235
Reviewable means it is understandable to the people who need to use it.

00:01:25.235 --> 00:01:28.530
Continuous monitoring improves when evidence quality is treated as part of the security

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:29.020
program,

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:30.640
not as an afterthought.
